  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 342 AM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nThe cold front is now east of the Northland this morning, with a\nband of post frontal light rain persisting over northwest\nWisconsin. A few hundredths or up to a 0.10 inch of additional\nrainfall is possible in that area. Northwest winds continue to\nbring cold air southward, and current temps are in the 30s for\nmost of the area, with lower 40s hanging on near the I-35\ncorridor and along the South Shore. \n\nAttention turns towards the potential for light snow as a 500mb\nshortwave trough propagates through the Upper Midwest today. As\n850mb temps drop to around -8 degC this morning, snow showers\nwill move southward into the Arrowhead, with some enhancement\nsoutheast of lakes near the International border, and lake\neffects showers from Lake Superior into north- central WI. Snow\naccumulations downwind of these lakes are expected to be \nlocalized. Within these localized areas, amounts in excess of 1\"\nare certainly possible with the NBM probability of exceedence \nfor 1\" or greater showing a 40-60% chance. Higher amounts above \n4\" are unlikely as there is only a 15% chance for these higher \namounts. There are similar probabilities and expected snowfall \namounts for the snowbelt regions of northern Iron County around \nthe Penokee Range. \n\nOutside of lake effect snow, there is a 50% or greater chance for at \nleast 0.1\" of snow across the MN Arrowhead along and north of the \nIron Range on Sunday through Sunday night as this shortwave \npropagates through the CWA. Hi-res model soundings show the \npotential for steep low-level lapse rates and modest MUCAPE up to 50 \nJ/kg, which could lead to brief and localized heavier snowfall \nrates. The main impact from these heavier snow showers is expected \nto be rapidly reduced visibility. While visibility may be briefly \nlower, true snow squall impacts with snow covered roads are unlikely \non Sunday afternoon as MnDOT RWIS sites currently report road sfc \ntemps in the mid 40s and temps are not forecast to consistently drop \nbelow freezing for a prolonged period until Sunday evening. By \nSunday evening, instability decreases which will limit the potential \nfor any snow squalls. However, this will be something to \nclosely monitor given the potential impacts to travel. \n  \nAfter lake effect snow comes to an end on Monday morning in Iron \nCounty, precipitation chances will be minimal to none across the CWA \nthrough the upcoming workweek. Sub-freezing temps stick around for \nmost early in the week on Monday through Tuesday night. Starting \nWednesday into next weekend, a diurnal pattern develops with high \ntemps in the 30s to low 40s and overnight low temps in the teens to \nmid 20s.",
